, \'community_connections\': 11, \'total_connections\': 20}, {\'agent_id\': 5, \'age\': 19, \'age_group\': \'Young Adult\', \'occupation\': \'Blue Collar\', \'risk_perception\': 0.110945287593398, \'initial_mask_wearing\': False, \'family_connections\': 3, \'work_school_connections\': 4, \'community_connections\': 18, \'total_connections\': 25}, {\'agent_id\': 6, \'age\': 27, \'age_group\': \'Young Adult\', \'occupation\': \'Student\', \'risk_perception\': 0.5098966417920571, \'initial_mask_wearing\': False, \'family_connections\': 1, \'work_school_connections\': 5, \'community_connections\': 15, \'total_connections\': 21}, {\'agent_id\': 7, \'age\': 21, \'age_group\': \'Young Adult\', \'occupation\': \'White Collar\', \'risk_perception\': 0.1772703794727445, \'initial_mask_wearing\': False, \'family_connections\': 2, \'work_school_connections\': 4, \'community_connections\': 13, \'total_connections\': 19}, {\'agent_id\': 8, \'age\': 27, \'age_group\': \'Young Adult\', \'occupation\': \'Blue Collar\', \'risk_perception\': 0.1982904719133444, \'initial_mask_wearing\': False, \'family_connections\': 3, \'work_school_connections\': 5, \'community_connections\': 14, \'total_connections\': 22}, {\'agent_id\': 9, \'age\': 31, \'age_group\': \'Young Adult\', \'occupation\': \'Student\', \'risk_perception\': 0.3762367882135499, \'initial_mask_wearing\': False, \'family_connections\': 2, \'work_school_connections\': 4, \'community_connections\': 18, \'total_connections\': 24}]\n\nSemantic summary of this file: \nConcise semantic metadata summary for agent_attributes.csv:\n\n- Overall structure/type:\n  - Tabular CSV; one record per agent.\n  - Primary key: agent_id (integer).\n  - Field types: integers (age, connection counts), categorical strings (age_group, occupation), float in [0,1] (risk_perception), boolean (initial_mask_wearing).\n\n- Relationships/nested elements:\n  - No nested structures; all flat fields.\n  - Implicit multilayer social network via degree counts:\n    - family_connections, work_school_connections, community_connections.\n    - total_connections = family + work/school + community (acts as a consistency check).\n  - No explicit edges, groups, or household/workplace identifiers; only per-layer degree constraints.\n\n- How to inform simulation entities/interactions:\n  - Agent initialization:\n    - Create one Agent per row with attributes: age, age_group, occupation, risk_perception, mask_wearing_state initialized from initial_mask_wearing.\n    - agent_id used to link to any generated networks or future datasets.\n  - Network construction:\n    - Build a multilayer graph (family, work/school, community) using the provided per-layer degrees (e.g., configuration model or group-based assignment) so each agent’s degree in each layer matches counts.\n    - Use total_connections and per-layer degrees to validate the generated network and to compute influence normalization.\n  - Behavior dynamics:\n    - risk_perception informs baseline propensity to wear masks or threshold sensitivity to social influence.\n    - Layered influence: weight neighbors by layer (e.g., stronger in family, moderate in work/school, weaker in community) when updating mask adoption.\n    - age_group and occupation can modulate contact rates, influence weights, or risk sensitivity to introduce heterogeneity.\n    - initial_mask_wearing sets seed adopters for diffusion.\n  - Data quality/constraints to enforce:\n    - Non-negative integer degrees; total equals sum of layer degrees.\n    - Booleans parsed correctly.\n    - Categorical fields standardized for policy or parameter mappings.\n\nFile: social_network.json (type: json)\n\nExample data of this file: \n{\'0\': {\'family\': [80, 500, 122], \'work_school\': [1, 998, 5, 994], \'community\': [879, 712, 900, 533, 624, 691, 69, 315, 379, 647, 847], \'all\': [80, 500, 122, 1, 998, 5, 994, 879, 712, 900, 533, 624, 691, 69, 315, 379, 647, 847]}, \'1\': {\'family\': [534, 441, 760], \'work_school\': [0, 5, 8, 998], \'community\': [729, 55, 955, 975, 453, 68, 246, 281, 400, 606, 900, 910], \'all\': [534, 441, 760, 0, 5, 8, 998, 729, 55, 955, 975, 453, 68, 246, 281, 400, 606, 900, 910]}, \'2\': {\'family\': [655, 771, 294, 206], \'work_school\': [15, 973, 49, 971, 951], \'community\': [995, 868, 780, 116, 111, 807, 630, 629, 76, 352, 430, 438, 846], \'all\': [655, 771, 294, 206, 15, 973, 49, 971, 951, 995, 868, 780, 116, 111, 807, 630, 629, 76, 352, 430, 438, 846]}, \'3\': {\'family\': [277, 968], \'work_school\': [6, 999, 9, 993], \'community\': [847, 579, 45, 523, 110, 123, 261, 264, 394, 602, 790, 960], \'all\': [277, 968, 6, 999, 9, 993, 847, 579, 45, 523, 110, 123, 261, 264, 394, 602, 790, 960]}, \'4\': {\'family\': [252, 320, 172, 438, 945], \'work_school\': [7, 997, 10, 996], \'community\': [652, 123, 898, 214, 939, 376, 76, 196, 231, 519, 566], \'all\': [252, 320, 172, 438, 945, 7, 997, 10, 996, 652, 123, 898, 214, 939, 376, 76, 196, 231, 519, 566]}, \'5\': {\'family\': [586, 42, 126], \'work_school\': [0, 1, 8, 301], \'community\': [753, 306, 733, 459, 788, 525, 27, 95, 312, 356, 361, 542, 574, 825, 837, 857, 903, 941], \'all\': [586, 42, 126, 0, 1, 8, 301, 753, 306, 733, 459, 788, 525, 27, 95, 312, 356, 361, 542, 574, 825, 837, 857, 903, 941]}, \'6\': {\'family\': [656], \'work_school\': [3, 9, 18, 999, 534], \'community\': [756, 897, 105, 161, 668, 376, 346, 844, 219, 246, 355, 624, 640, 646, 777], \'all\': [656, 3, 9, 18, 999, 534, 756, 897, 105, 161, 668, 376, 346, 844, 219, 246, 355, 624, 640, 646, 777]}, \'7\': {\'family\': [641, 546], \'work_school\': [4, 10, 166, 407], \'community\': [128, 875, 210, 454, 999, 430, 86, 126, 175, 257, 358, 517, 922], \'all\': [641, 546, 4, 10, 166, 407, 128, 875, 210, 454, 999, 430, 86, 126, 175, 257, 358, 517, 922]}, \'8\': {\'family\': [713, 923, 930], \'work_school\': [1, 5, 11, 14, 789], \'community\': [256, 861, 835, 490, 354, 81, 154, 346, 369, 483, 620, 786, 889, 971], \'all\': [713, 923, 930, 1, 5, 11, 14, 789, 256, 861, 835, 490, 354, 81, 154, 346, 369, 483, 620, 786, 889, 971]}, \'9\': {\'family\': [340, 341], \'work_school\': [3, 6, 18, 19], \'community\': [524, 180, 92, 277, 388, 739, 779, 82, 255, 298, 335, 357, 395, 415, 557, 606, 658, 831], \'all\': [340, 341, 3, 6, 18, 19, 524, 180, 92, 277, 388, 739, 779, 82, 255, 298, 335, 357, 395, 415, 557, 606, 658, 831]}}\n\nSemantic summary of this file: \nSemantic metadata summary for social_network.json\n\n- Overall structure and type\n  - JSON object keyed by agent IDs (strings), each mapping to an object of contact lists.\n  - Values are arrays of integer agent IDs (0–999 range in sample), representing adjacency lists.\n  - Multi-layer (multiplex) network: layers = family, work_school, community, plus all (union).\n\n- Relationships and nesting\n  - Each agent has up to four fields:\n    - family: close-tie contacts (household/kin).\n    - work_school: workplace/school contacts.\n    - community: broader community contacts.\n    - all: union of the three layers (redundant; should equal set(family ∪ work_school ∪ community)).\n  - Edges are listed from the ego’s perspective; reciprocity is not guaranteed for every tie (i.e., may be directed in the raw data). No weights or timestamps provided.\n  - Potential data considerations: duplicate IDs across lists, possible missing keys per agent, and string vs integer ID types (keys are strings; neighbor IDs are integers).\n\n- How this informs simulation entities and interactions\n  - Agents: one per node ID; state includes mask-wearing status, propensity/susceptibility parameters, and possibly layer-specific responsiveness.\n  - Interactions: influence/contagion occurs over edges with layer attributes. Use different contact frequencies or influence strengths per layer (e.g., family > work_school > community).\n  - Network handling:\n    - Prefer constructing a multiplex graph with separate layers; compute all dynamically to avoid double counting.\n    - Decide on directed vs undirected: treat as directed as given or symmetrize if modeling mutual influence is desired.\n    - Optionally assign default weights by layer since none are provided.\n  - Dynamics: schedule interactions by context (e.g., daily family + work/school, periodic community), apply adoption/update rules (threshold or probabilistic) over neighbors in each layer, and seed initial mask adopters as needed.\n\nFile: train_data.csv (type: csv)\n\nExample data of this file: \n[{\'day\': 0, \'agent_id\': 0, \'wearing_mask\': False, \'received_info\': False}, {\'day\': 1, \'agent_id\': 0, \'wearing_mask\': True, \'received_info\': False}, {\'day\': 2, \'agent_id\': 0, \'wearing_mask\': True, \'received_info\': False}, {\'day\': 3, \'agent_id\': 0, \'wearing_mask\': False, \'received_info\': False}, {\'day\': 4, \'agent_id\': 0, \'wearing_mask\': False, \'received_info\': False}, {\'day\': 5, \'agent_id\': 0, \'wearing_mask\': True, \'received_info\': False}, {\'day\': 6, \'agent_id\': 0, \'wearing_mask\': False, \'received_info\': False}, {\'day\': 7, \'agent_id\': 0, \'wearing_mask\': True, \'received_info\': False}, {\'day\': 8, \'agent_id\': 0, \'wearing_mask\': False, \'received_info\': False}, {\'day\': 9, \'agent_id\': 0, \'wearing_mask\': False, \'received_info\': False}]\n\nSemantic summary of this file: \nSemantic metadata summary for train_data.csv\n\n- Overall data structure and type\n  - Format: Tabular CSV (sample shown in JSON-like form).\n  - Columns: day (int), agent_id (int), wearing_mask (bool), received_info (bool).\n  - Granularity: One row per agent per day (panel time series).\n  - Primary key: Composite (day, agent_id).\n  - Time index: day is a discrete tick.\n\n- Relationships or nested elements\n  - Flat structure; no nesting.\n  - Rows are grouped by agent_id across sequential days, forming a longitudinal record of agent states.\n  - No explicit social network/edges; “received_info” is an agent-level exposure flag, but the source (peer/broadcast) is not specified in this file.\n\n- How this data should inform simulation entities or interactions\n  - Agent state variables per tick:\n    - wearing_mask: binary behavioral state.\n    - received_info: binary exposure/intervention state.\n  - Transition modeling:\n    - Use sequences to estimate baseline transition probabilities P(wear_t | wear_{t-1}) when received_info = 0 (adoption/persistence/abandon rates).\n    - When data contains received_info = 1, estimate effect of information on adoption/retention P(wear_t | wear_{t-1}, received_info_t).\n  - Initialization and scheduling:\n    - Map day to simulation ticks; initialize each agent’s state at day 0 and update per tick.\n  - Network dynamics:\n    - Since edges are absent, import or generate a social graph separately.\n    - Use the learned baseline transitions as individual propensities; modulate by peer influence computed from the network (e.g., fraction of masked neighbors) and by received_info.\n  - Calibration/learning:\n    - Fit a simple Markov/logistic model at the agent-day level; include lagged wearing_mask and received_info as predictors.\n    - Use learned parameters to drive stochastic state updates in the multi-agent simulation.\n  - Notes from sample:\n    - Example shows fluctuation in wearing_mask without received_info, useful for baseline switching estimates.\n    - If received_info=1 is rare in the dataset, the info-effect may need regularization or external priors.\n\nBased on the provided task description and data summaries, please analyze the data and provide guidelines for simulation model construction.\n\nYour analysis must cover:\n1) Overall simulation design: State the primary objective mode (choose from: "Descriptive" | "Predictive" | "What-if (Interventional)" | "Counterfactual" | "Policy Evaluation") and justify the choice. Specify how the simulation initializes from inputs and what artifacts it outputs after execution.\n2) Scale & Granularity: Specify time step, spatial resolution (or explicitly "non-spatial"), and population size (agents), with brief rationale.\n3) Agent Archetypes: Define the agent unit and roles; list static attributes and dynamic states; explain how the input data construct static attributes and how dynamic states update from data-derived signals.\n4) Interaction Topology: Describe how agents interact; explain how to build interactions (layers/edges/protocols) from the input data.\n5) Information propagation: Clarify whether information diffusion exists, its topology/mechanism, and how to parameterize/drive it using inputs.\n6) Exogenous Signals: Identify any external signals/interventions, how they are derived from inputs, and how they affect agent decisions.\n7) Action Decision Policy: Describe actions taken by agents and the decision policy mapping observations/signals to actions; include role-specific inputs and policy forms.\n8) Holdout: Propose a training / validation data split plan if needed. For time series, prefer first 80% of days as train and last 20% as validation; state exact ranges or the rule to compute them.\n9) Simulation Evaluation: Define evaluation metrics and how to compare simulator output artifacts against validation ground truth.\n10) Calibratable Parameters: List tunable parameters with bounds/ranges that will be used for configuring the simulation.\n\nProvide your response in the following JSON format (valid JSON only, no extra text):\n\n{\n  "overall_simulation_design": {\n    "objective_mode": "Descriptive|Predictive|What-if (Interventional)|Counterfactual|Policy Evaluation",\n    "rationale": "Why this mode fits the task and data",\n    "initialization": "How the simulation starts from inputs (states, seeds, signals)",\n    "outputs": ["List of simulation artifacts to produce (e.g., per-agent states by day, aggregate adoption curves, logs)"]\n  },\n  "scale_granularity": {\n    "time_step": "seconds|minutes|hours|days",\n    "spatial_resolution": "non-spatial|grid|POI|road network|other:<...>",\n    "population_size": "integer or description tied to data",\n    "rationale": "Why these scales are appropriate"\n  },\n  "agent_archetypes": {\n    "unit": "e.g., resident",\n    "roles": ["role1", "role2"],\n    "static_attributes": ["list of static fields mapped from inputs"],\n    "dynamic_states": ["list of dynamic states tracked over time"],\n    "construction_from_data": "How input data map to static attributes (IDs, demographics, risk, initial states, etc.)",\n    "update_from_data": "How dynamic states update using observations/signals derived from data"\n  },\n  "interaction_topology": {\n    "topology": "graph|hybrid|broadcast|market|spatial",\n    "layers": ["e.g., family", "work_school", "community", "all"],\n    "protocol": "p2p on layers|group broadcast|platform-level rules",\n    "construction_from_data": "How to build edges/layers from social_network / counts / metadata"\n  },\n  "information_propagation": {\n    "exists": true,\n    "topology": "layer(s) used for diffusion",\n    "mechanism": "e.g., neighbor fraction, threshold, broadcast rate",\n    "construction_from_data": "Which file/field drives info intensity or schedule",\n    "parameters": {"names": ["e.g., info_rate", "neighbor_weight"], "notes": "how to estimate or tune"}\n  },\n  "exogenous_signals": [\n    {\n      "name": "signal_name",\n      "construction_from_data": "which file/field defines or constrains it",\n      "observability": "which roles can observe; delay/noise if any",\n      "effect_on_agents": "how it enters decision function",\n      "bounds": "[low, high]"\n    }\n  ],\n  "action_decision_policy": {\n    "by_role": {\n      "role1": {\n        "inputs": ["observations/signals used by this role"],\n        "policy_form": "e.g., logistic with peer/self/policy terms; thresholds; inertia",\n        "parameters": ["list of per-role parameters"]\n      },\n      "role2": {\n        "inputs": ["..."],\n        "policy_form": "...",\n        "parameters": ["..."]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  "holdout_plan": {\n    "method": "temporal_holdout|rolling_backtest",\n    "train_range": "e.g., day 0–23 or first 80% rule",\n    "validation_range": "e.g., day 24–29 or last 20% rule",\n    "notes": "any agent-level holdout or rolling details"\n  },\n  "simulation_evaluation": {\n    "metrics": [\n      {"name": "RMSE_aggregate", "definition": "RMSE between simulated and observed daily adoption rates"},\n      {"name": "MAE_aggregate", "definition": "MAE between curves"},\n      {"name": "Brier", "definition": "Brier score for per-agent probabilities if available"},\n      {"name": "TransitionFit", "definition": "error on P10/P11/P01 rates vs observed"}\n    ],\n    "comparison_method": "how to compute metrics on validation set and report (tables/plots)"\n  },\n  "calibratable_parameters": [\n    {\n      "name": "influence_weight_family|work_school|community",\n      "range_bounds": "[0,1] or rationale",\n      "source": "constrained by network layers / train_data dynamics",\n      "notes": "tie to topology and adoption speed"\n    },\n    {\n      "name": "threshold|inertia|policy_weight|info_rate|memory_decay",\n      "range_bounds": "[0,1] or problem-appropriate bounds",\n      "source": "estimated from transitions; refined via optimization",\n      "notes": "regularize to avoid overfitting"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\nReturn only valid JSON that can be parsed. Do not include any other explanation or text outside the JSON.\n'}]}], 'max_output_tokens': 10000, 'model': 'gpt-5-2025-08-07'}}
