2025-09-11 01:14:12,814 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/responses', 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-91dc04fe-f287-4faa-ab06-6891ea572e5f', 'json_data': {'input': [{'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'input_text', 'text': 'You are the Model Planning Agent in a system that generates social simulations. Your job is to design the structure and approach for a simulation based on the task requirements and data analysis.\n\nTask Specification:\n{\n  "title": "Simulation Task",\n  "description": "Develop a multi-agent simulation system that models the spread of mask-wearing behavior through social networks.",\n  "simulation_focus": [\n    "Predict agents\' mask-wearing behavior over day 30 - day 39 (last ten days) based on their attributes, social connections, and past behavior",\n    "Model information propagation and behavioral changes triggered by social influence and government intervention starting on Day 10"\n  ],\n  "data_folder": "data_fitting/mask_adoption_data/",\n  "data_files": {\n    "agent_attributes.csv": "Contains demographic and behavioral attributes of each agent, including age, occupation, risk perception, and social connection counts",\n    "social_network.json": "Contains structured data representing a social network. Each entry corresponds to a user (identified by a unique integer ID), and the values describe the different types of relationships that user has with others. Data Structure: The top-level structure is a dictionary ({} in JSON). Keys: Each key is a unique user ID (as an integer, e.g., 0, 1, 2, etc.). Values: Each value is another dictionary with the following fields: \\"family\\": a list of user IDs representing family connections. \\"work_school\\": a list of user IDs representing colleagues or schoolmates. \\"community\\": a list of user IDs representing community-level acquaintances. \\"all\\": a merged list of all user IDs connected to the user across the three categories above.",\n    "train_data.csv": "Time series data for the first 30 days, used for training the model"\n  },\n  "evaluation_metrics": {\n    "RMSE": {\n      "description": "Root Mean Square Error measuring overall predictive accuracy of mask adoption rates",\n      "interpretation": "Directly interpretable as percentage deviations due to the 0-1 range",\n      "formula": "RMSE = sqrt(sum((predicted_rate - actual_rate)^2) / n)"\n    }\n  },\n  "data_analysis_result": {\n    "overall_simulation_design": {\n      "objective_mode": "Policy Evaluation",\n      "rationale": "The goal is to model and compare how mask-wearing behavior diffuses under different interventions (e.g., information campaigns, mandates) over an observed multilayer social network. The data provide agent attributes, explicit edges, and longitudinal behavior needed to calibrate and then evaluate policies.",\n      "initialization": "1) Load agent_attributes.csv to create agents with demographics, risk_perception, and initial_mask_wearing. 2) Load social_network.json to build a multiplex graph (family, work_school, community) and union for checks. 3) If train_data.csv is available, set day 0 mask states from it (overriding initial_mask_wearing) and use its history to calibrate transition/logit parameters; also set received_info when provided. 4) Set random seed and scenario schedule (e.g., mandate on/off, campaign intensity) for the run.",\n      "outputs": [\n        "Per-agent daily mask_wearing states and probabilities",\n        "Aggregate adoption curves by day and by layer-context (family/work/community exposure)",\n        "Influence decomposition logs (contributions of family/work/community/info to decisions)",\n        "Policy impact summaries (difference-in-adoption vs baseline)",\n        "Calibration diagnostics (fitted parameters, likelihood, residuals)",\n        "Validation metrics and plots data (aggregate vs observed)"\n      ]\n    },\n    "scale_granularity": {\n      "time_step": "days",\n      "spatial_resolution": "non-spatial",\n      "population_size": "Number of unique agent_ids in the intersection of agent_attributes.csv and social_network.json keys (expected ~1000 in sample)",\n      "rationale": "train_data.csv is indexed by day; social interactions and behavioral updates are reasonable at daily cadence. No spatial fields are provided; social layers capture relevant heterogeneity."\n    },\n    "agent_archetypes": {\n      "unit": "person",\n      "roles": [\n        "Family tie participant",\n        "Worker/Student",\n        "Community member"\n      ],\n      "static_attributes": [\n        "agent_id",\n        "age",\n        "age_group",\n        "occupation",\n        "risk_perception",\n        "degree_family",\n        "degree_work_school",\n        "degree_community",\n        "total_connections"\n      ],\n      "dynamic_states": [\n        "wearing_mask (bool)",\n        "mask_intent (probability)",\n        "received_info (bool)",\n        "perceived_norm_family (0-1)",\n        "perceived_norm_work_school (0-1)",\n        "perceived_norm_community (0-1)"\n      ],\n      "construction_from_data": "Map each CSV row in agent_attributes.csv to an agent. Degrees per layer come from social_network.json lists (count unique neighbors per layer) or from agent_attributes.csv for validation. Initialize wearing_mask from train_data.csv day 0 when available; otherwise use initial_mask_wearing.",\n      "update_from_data": "Estimate/logit-calibrate transitions using train_data.csv (wear_t vs wear_{t-1} and received_info_t). During simulation, update perceived_norm_* as (fraction of neighbors wearing) per layer each day, then update mask_intent via calibrated policy and draw wearing_mask ~ Bernoulli(mask_intent). received_info is set exogenously from scenarios or from train_data when simulating the observed period."\n    },\n    "interaction_topology": {\n      "topology": "graph",\n      "layers": [\n        "family",\n        "work_school",\n        "community"\n      ],\n      "protocol": "p2p on layers with layer-specific influence weights and contact frequencies",\n      "construction_from_data": "For each agent_id key in social_network.json, create layer-specific adjacency lists. Treat edges as undirected by symmetrizing: add (u,v) if either list contains the other; deduplicate neighbors. Validate layer degrees against agent_attributes.csv counts; if mismatches > tolerance, prefer network.json edges and log discrepancies. Do not double-count neighbors across layers when computing per-layer fractions."\n    },\n    "information_propagation": {\n      "exists": true,\n      "topology": "Broadcast plus optional peer relays over all layers",\n      "mechanism": "Exogenous broadcast sets received_info=1 for targeted agents per day with probability campaign_intensity; optional peer relay: an informed agent passes info to a neighbor with probability gamma_info per edge per day. Adoption depends on received_info via a positive shift in the logit of mask_intent.",\n      "construction_from_data": "Use received_info in train_data.csv directly for calibration/observed runs. For counterfactual/policy runs, drive info exposure via scenario parameters (campaign_intensity schedule and targeting rules).",\n      "parameters": {\n        "names": [\n          "campaign_intensity",\n          "gamma_info",\n          "info_weight"\n        ],\n        "notes": "campaign_intensity in [0,1] is daily probability of exposure for targeted cohorts; gamma_info in [0,1] is per-edge relay probability; info_weight is the logit coefficient linking received_info to mask adoption, calibrated from train_data and refined in validation."\n      }\n    },\n    "exogenous_signals": [\n      {\n        "name": "received_info",\n        "construction_from_data": "train_data.csv received_info (used when available); else scenario-driven via campaign_intensity and targeting",\n        "observability": "Observed by the focal agent immediately (no delay) on the day received",\n        "effect_on_agents": "Adds info_weight to the adoption logit on that day (and optionally decays over time if memory_decay<1)",\n        "bounds": "[0,1]"\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "mask_mandate",\n        "construction_from_data": "Not in provided files; define as scenario schedule (0/1 by day) for policy evaluation",\n        "observability": "All agents observe same-day; compliance heterogeneity via occupation/age modifiers",\n        "effect_on_agents": "Adds mandate_weight to the adoption logit; may also increase effective peer weights due to enforcement in work/school",\n        "bounds": "[0,1]"\n      }\n    ],\n    "action_decision_policy": {\n      "by_role": {\n        "Student": {\n          "inputs": [\n            "mask_{t-1}",\n            "risk_perception",\n            "perceived_norm_family",\n            "perceived_norm_work_school",\n            "perceived_norm_community",\n            "received_info_t",\n            "mask_mandate_t",\n            "age_group"\n          ],\n          "policy_form": "Logistic: P(mask_t=1)=sigmoid(b0 + b_prev*mask_{t-1} + b_risk*risk + wF*perceived_norm_family + wW*perceived_norm_work_school + wC*perceived_norm_community + b_info*received_info_t + b_man*mask_mandate_t + age_dummies)",\n          "parameters": [\n            "b0",\n            "b_prev",\n            "b_risk",\n            "wF",\n            "wW",\n            "wC",\n            "b_info",\n            "b_man",\n            "age_dummies"\n          ]\n        },\n        "Blue Collar": {\n          "inputs": [\n            "mask_{t-1}",\n            "risk_perception",\n            "perceived_norm_* (all layers)",\n            "received_info_t",\n            "mask_mandate_t",\n            "age_group"\n          ],\n          "policy_form": "Same logistic form; allow role-specific layer weights (e.g., higher wW for work-centric contacts)",\n          "parameters": [\n            "b0_bc",\n            "b_prev_bc",\n            "b_risk_bc",\n            "wF_bc",\n            "wW_bc",\n            "wC_bc",\n            "b_info_bc",\n            "b_man_bc",\n            "age_dummies"\n          ]\n        },\n        "White Collar": {\n          "inputs": [\n            "mask_{t-1}",\n            "risk_perception",\n            "perceived_norm_*",\n            "received_info_t",\n            "mask_mandate_t",\n            "age_group"\n          ],\n          "policy_form": "Same logistic; potentially higher sensitivity to received_info and community norms",\n          "parameters": [\n            "b0_wc",\n            "b_prev_wc",\n            "b_risk_wc",\n            "wF_wc",\n            "wW_wc",\n            "wC_wc",\n            "b_info_wc",\n            "b_man_wc",\n            "age_dummies"\n          ]\n        },\n        "Other": {\n          "inputs": [\n            "mask_{t-1}",\n            "risk_perception",\n            "perceived_norm_*",\n            "received_info_t",\n            "mask_mandate_t",\n            "age_group"\n          ],\n          "policy_form": "Same logistic; used for occupations outside Student/Blue Collar/White Collar",\n          "parameters": [\n            "b0_o",\n            "b_prev_o",\n            "b_risk_o",\n            "wF_o",\n            "wW_o",\n            "wC_o",\n            "b_info_o",\n            "b_man_o",\n            "age_dummies"\n          ]\n        }\n      }\n    },\n    "holdout_plan": {\n      "method": "temporal_holdout",\n      "train_range": "First 80% of days per agent: days [0, floor(0.8*T)-1]",\n      "validation_range": "Last 20% of days per agent: days [floor(0.8*T), T-1]",\n      "notes": "Calibrate parameters on train_range using observed mask and received_info. Initialize simulation for validation at day floor(0.8*T) with the last observed mask state per agent, then simulate forward using the network and any provided received_info during validation (if available); otherwise use scenario settings."\n    },\n    "simulation_evaluation": {\n      "metrics": [\n        {\n          "name": "RMSE_aggregate",\n          "definition": "RMSE between simulated and observed daily fraction of agents wearing masks on validation days"\n        },\n        {\n          "name": "MAE_aggregate",\n          "definition": "MAE between aggregate adoption curves over validation days"\n        },\n        {\n          "name": "Brier",\n          "definition": "Mean Brier score of predicted per-agent probabilities vs observed wearing_mask on validation days"\n        },\n        {\n          "name": "TransitionFit",\n          "definition": "Absolute error of simulated vs observed transition rates: P01 (adopt), P11 (stay), P10 (drop), P00 (stay off) over validation"\n        }\n      ],\n      "comparison_method": "Run simulation over validation_range with calibrated parameters. Compute aggregate adoption by day and per-agent probabilities. Compare to validation observations from train_data.csv using the listed metrics; report summaries and layer-stratified errors (family/work/community exposure bins) for diagnostic plots/tables."\n    },\n    "calibratable_parameters": [\n      {\n        "name": "wF (influence_weight_family)",\n        "range_bounds": "[0.0, 3.0]",\n        "source": "Layered peer effects; tuned to match adoption speed and TransitionFit",\n        "notes": "Higher family weight strengthens within-household conformity"\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "wW (influence_weight_work_school)",\n        "range_bounds": "[0.0, 3.0]",\n        "source": "Work/school contacts; calibrated from role-specific dynamics",\n        "notes": "Often strong for Students and Blue Collar"\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "wC (influence_weight_community)",\n        "range_bounds": "[0.0, 3.0]",\n        "source": "Community ties; typically weaker but numerous",\n        "notes": "Controls broad diffusion speed"\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "b0 (baseline_bias)",\n        "range_bounds": "[-5.0, 5.0]",\n        "source": "Intercept of logistic adoption",\n        "notes": "Sets baseline adoption propensity absent influence"\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "b_prev (inertia)",\n        "range_bounds": "[0.0, 5.0]",\n        "source": "Estimated from P11/P00 persistence in train_data",\n        "notes": "Captures habit/stickiness of prior-day behavior"\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "b_risk (risk_weight)",\n        "range_bounds": "[-5.0, 5.0]",\n        "source": "Maps risk_perception to adoption",\n        "notes": "Positive values: higher perceived risk increases adoption"\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "b_info (info_weight)",\n        "range_bounds": "[0.0, 5.0]",\n        "source": "Effect of received_info on adoption; estimated where available",\n        "notes": "Can decay over time if memory_decay<1"\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "b_man (mandate_weight)",\n        "range_bounds": "[0.0, 5.0]",\n        "source": "Scenario/policy effect magnitude",\n        "notes": "May vary by occupation via multipliers"\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "age_group_multipliers",\n        "range_bounds": "each in [-2.0, 2.0]",\n        "source": "Age heterogeneity from train_data stratification",\n        "notes": "Additive or multiplicative adjustments to logit"\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "occupation_multipliers",\n        "range_bounds": "each in [-2.0, 2.0]",\n        "source": "Role-specific responsiveness",\n        "notes": "Adjust baseline or layer weights by occupation"\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "memory_decay",\n        "range_bounds": "[0.0, 1.0]",\n        "source": "Controls exponential smoothing of perceived norms across days",\n        "notes": "0 uses only current day; 1 accumulates full history"\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "gamma_info (peer_info_relay)",\n        "range_bounds": "[0.0, 1.0]",\n        "source": "Information diffusion rate on edges",\n        "notes": "Used only when enabling peer relays"\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "campaign_intensity",\n        "range_bounds": "[0.0, 1.0]",\n        "source": "Exogenous broadcast exposure rate",\n        "notes": "Policy lever for what-if experiments"\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "temperature (decision_noise)",\n        "range_bounds": "[0.1, 5.0]",\n        "source": "Scales stochasticity in decisions",\n        "notes": "Higher temperature increases randomness"\n      }\n    ],\n    "file_summaries": [\n      {\n        "file_name": "agent_attributes.csv",\n        "file_type": "csv",\n        "semantic_summary": "Concise semantic metadata summary for agent_attributes.csv:\\n\\n- Overall structure/type:\\n  - Tabular CSV; one record per agent.\\n  - Primary key: agent_id (integer).\\n  - Field types: integers (age, connection counts), categorical strings (age_group, occupation), float in [0,1] (risk_perception), boolean (initial_mask_wearing).\\n\\n- Relationships/nested elements:\\n  - No nested structures; all flat fields.\\n  - Implicit multilayer social network via degree counts:\\n    - family_connections, work_school_connections, community_connections.\\n    - total_connections = family + work/school + community (acts as a consistency check).\\n  - No explicit edges, groups, or household/workplace identifiers; only per-layer degree constraints.\\n\\n- How to inform simulation entities/interactions:\\n  - Agent initialization:\\n    - Create one Agent per row with attributes: age, age_group, occupation, risk_perception, mask_wearing_state initialized from initial_mask_wearing.\\n    - agent_id used to link to any generated networks or future datasets.\\n  - Network construction:\\n    - Build a multilayer graph (family, work/school, community) using the provided per-layer degrees (e.g., configuration model or group-based assignment) so each agent\\u2019s degree in each layer matches counts.\\n    - Use total_connections and per-layer degrees to validate the generated network and to compute influence normalization.\\n  - Behavior dynamics:\\n    - risk_perception informs baseline propensity to wear masks or threshold sensitivity to social influence.\\n    - Layered influence: weight neighbors by layer (e.g., stronger in family, moderate in work/school, weaker in community) when updating mask adoption.\\n    - age_group and occupation can modulate contact rates, influence weights, or risk sensitivity to introduce heterogeneity.\\n    - initial_mask_wearing sets seed adopters for diffusion.\\n  - Data quality/constraints to enforce:\\n    - Non-negative integer degrees; total equals sum of layer degrees.\\n    - Booleans parsed correctly.\\n    - Categorical fields standardized for policy or parameter mappings."\n      },\n      {\n        "file_name": "social_network.json",\n        "file_type": "json",\n        "semantic_summary": "Semantic metadata summary for social_network.json\\n\\n- Overall structure and type\\n  - JSON object keyed by agent IDs (strings), each mapping to an object of contact lists.\\n  - Values are arrays of integer agent IDs (0\\u2013999 range in sample), representing adjacency lists.\\n  - Multi-layer (multiplex) network: layers = family, work_school, community, plus all (union).\\n\\n- Relationships and nesting\\n  - Each agent has up to four fields:\\n    - family: close-tie contacts (household/kin).\\n    - work_school: workplace/school contacts.\\n    - community: broader community contacts.\\n    - all: union of the three layers (redundant; should equal set(family \\u222a work_school \\u222a community)).\\n  - Edges are listed from the ego\\u2019s perspective; reciprocity is not guaranteed for every tie (i.e., may be directed in the raw data). No weights or timestamps provided.\\n  - Potential data considerations: duplicate IDs across lists, possible missing keys per agent, and string vs integer ID types (keys are strings; neighbor IDs are integers).\\n\\n- How this informs simulation entities and interactions\\n  - Agents: one per node ID; state includes mask-wearing status, propensity/susceptibility parameters, and possibly layer-specific responsiveness.\\n  - Interactions: influence/contagion occurs over edges with layer attributes. Use different contact frequencies or influence strengths per layer (e.g., family > work_school > community).\\n  - Network handling:\\n    - Prefer constructing a multiplex graph with separate layers; compute all dynamically to avoid double counting.\\n    - Decide on directed vs undirected: treat as directed as given or symmetrize if modeling mutual influence is desired.\\n    - Optionally assign default weights by layer since none are provided.\\n  - Dynamics: schedule interactions by context (e.g., daily family + work/school, periodic community), apply adoption/update rules (threshold or probabilistic) over neighbors in each layer, and seed initial mask adopters as needed."\n      },\n      {\n        "file_name": "train_data.csv",\n        "file_type": "csv",\n        "semantic_summary": "Semantic metadata summary for train_data.csv\\n\\n- Overall data structure and type\\n  - Format: Tabular CSV (sample shown in JSON-like form).\\n  - Columns: day (int), agent_id (int), wearing_mask (bool), received_info (bool).\\n  - Granularity: One row per agent per day (panel time series).\\n  - Primary key: Composite (day, agent_id).\\n  - Time index: day is a discrete tick.\\n\\n- Relationships or nested elements\\n  - Flat structure; no nesting.\\n  - Rows are grouped by agent_id across sequential days, forming a longitudinal record of agent states.\\n  - No explicit social network/edges; \\u201creceived_info\\u201d is an agent-level exposure flag, but the source (peer/broadcast) is not specified in this file.\\n\\n- How this data should inform simulation entities or interactions\\n  - Agent state variables per tick:\\n    - wearing_mask: binary behavioral state.\\n    - received_info: binary exposure/intervention state.\\n  - Transition modeling:\\n    - Use sequences to estimate baseline transition probabilities P(wear_t | wear_{t-1}) when received_info = 0 (adoption/persistence/abandon rates).\\n    - When data contains received_info = 1, estimate effect of information on adoption/retention P(wear_t | wear_{t-1}, received_info_t).\\n  - Initialization and scheduling:\\n    - Map day to simulation ticks; initialize each agent\\u2019s state at day 0 and update per tick.\\n  - Network dynamics:\\n    - Since edges are absent, import or generate a social graph separately.\\n    - Use the learned baseline transitions as individual propensities; modulate by peer influence computed from the network (e.g., fraction of masked neighbors) and by received_info.\\n  - Calibration/learning:\\n    - Fit a simple Markov/logistic model at the agent-day level; include lagged wearing_mask and received_info as predictors.\\n    - Use learned parameters to drive stochastic state updates in the multi-agent simulation.\\n  - Notes from sample:\\n    - Example shows fluctuation in wearing_mask without received_info, useful for baseline switching estimates.\\n    - If received_info=1 is rare in the dataset, the info-effect may need regularization or external priors."\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  "schemas": {\n    "agent_attributes.csv": {\n      "file_type": "csv",\n      "row_count": 1000,\n      "column_count": 10,\n      "columns": [\n        "agent_id",\n        "age",\n        "age_group",\n        "occupation",\n        "risk_perception",\n        "initial_mask_wearing",\n        "family_connections",\n        "work_school_connections",\n        "community_connections",\n        "total_connections"\n      ],\n      "data_info": "<class \'pandas.core.frame.DataFrame\'>\\nRangeIndex: 1000 entries, 0 to 999\\nData columns (total 10 columns):\\n #   Column                   Non-Null Count  Dtype  \\n---  ------                   --------------  -----  \\n 0   agent_id                 1000 non-nul